#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "b0707ce1-a0fe-5749-9742-4768621332a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5605" :question/problem "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n![](images/b1/b121e46eacd73987eb2d088f4b3904d293e267dd3a005cced0bfd85f55c5b300.svg)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/b9/b97a40ce65d174e23907ba00478f49f633ce1b2bf9fcb5fa3fb7627ed1e27d11.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/a2/a27de99267f5b34b0202c43b984c199264fd2b4808add80c2856bed984ab14f0.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =1,$ we get \n![](images/61/61d0d2e59d71dfc71df3bb0604165a54d52fc483196bd452a03a02cb33d0d9e3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/1a/1acdb041f910f83f6cd7d17194e8ce25bc7e82020b55559ff5889b71e052a4c0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/b5/b50817ce9b7984b26e5099dfab0a6205dec82ff90d7069a87901dc92098ec905.svg)\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t),$ or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=4{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "58c1b360-e67c-5074-919b-18fb5dbab146" :question/math-academy-id "e-5606" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)+7{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n\n![](images/99/99512ca0bae468975d098cf0789926fe6e8f4ef608693129fa57a3dba02c870b.svg)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-1,$ we get \n![](images/45/459df7b451dfbcef264fdf3a1af40f05526529934bb2617a8f43599ee56d27d4.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/b4/b4b518f6ec5abed1cefa88d9de3b42ec18a2553426667def4d7eb4a9964a7e56.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =3,$ we get \n![](images/25/2555ccf5ffe3735fc7cbcd5c5cdbab58b5bf63921ede47ee10ba1192a629b6ba.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/26/26da2e9864ffdb4795701703b58edbee58f73b131cddc7b229b1dc2e007b1678.svg)\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c7bde1c8-25e8-5573-9ac4-feedd40e122f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5974" :question/problem "Let ![](images/d6/d66afaa2e1560c9fb73e9d839b436717aebdf5ff2a7eafe09f7d3a429eacd94c.svg) and ![](images/c6/c69f65b8989accd6404431ec918e57c3f7853cd198f02507446e08dc8e433f91.svg) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a797ac7-33c4-512f-90f8-59eb16783f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-2{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402030} {:answer/id #uuid "54d062ef-f5a7-554f-9aec-32b3e4d45203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=-{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402031} {:answer/id #uuid "a9817443-5f2f-5697-b9b4-facc30ec893f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402032} {:answer/id #uuid "bb06d79b-0719-5c04-a4f5-69705749bb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=2{u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=2{u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402033} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f2d3e6-cd5c-5ab7-a220-8d6563dfe260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9817443-5f2f-5697-b9b4-facc30ec893f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402032} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c339bd-cf81-5ede-a22f-a63cc71054fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c347aa2-fe8e-5437-94e5-a44fd44640d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-324455" :question/problem "Find the substitution that will decouple the following system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024debe0-5fb3-5d92-b541-9a194a2b2146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299941} {:answer/id #uuid "891f8a2d-5509-5159-b687-e6c372ceeab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300652} {:answer/id #uuid "9283efc0-a1cb-5b14-aa96-6829ea180b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300696} {:answer/id #uuid "bc927e09-c086-5ed6-a3ce-9e4f8fed4928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300905} {:answer/id #uuid "f59d8bbc-9194-5ce5-acfc-e3800e7b28b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9283efc0-a1cb-5b14-aa96-6829ea180b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300696} :answer-field/id #uuid "53cd92a2-c50d-591c-a4c9-7497aca4b5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5119bc-30ba-5ab9-9edb-058e36254603" :question/math-academy-id "q-327974" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129306 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec56082-7fc8-5f94-b44a-5cb5efe5d2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413702} {:answer/id #uuid "54af6a24-5d99-5408-a766-dca48f108865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413705} {:answer/id #uuid "8da94f97-391c-5f2d-9aa6-f93861ebaae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413707} {:answer/id #uuid "9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413709} {:answer/id #uuid "b8299918-b9e8-5cbd-b68a-28bb2aaef1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413709} :answer-field/id #uuid "d472becc-17da-5ab2-96b6-76c458eb3ce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b90d6d2e-cbeb-5361-979a-20fa4635915b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327993" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129307 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04bcc08f-6df3-5da3-a584-9f0a56dba8f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413697} {:answer/id #uuid "054c9cf6-e5e5-5a1d-822d-f6804209660e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413698} {:answer/id #uuid "309d74bd-887c-5635-863d-c7f4529c284e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413700} {:answer/id #uuid "33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413701} {:answer/id #uuid "536f5988-b95f-5a22-8bfe-686e8a9dd306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413701} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b3fc9a-3397-5555-bc9f-987194333d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30504f26-ffc2-5a3c-bc4c-1549ba4e1d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39052" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8bfb79-cfc3-5844-8f84-8e979ddac966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345432} {:answer/id #uuid "108ce4cb-6473-5d9b-8e0e-8975aff13639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345492} {:answer/id #uuid "2fecb22e-9b78-5fb4-99aa-45ef0f590541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345776} {:answer/id #uuid "70f3cbf1-3fa6-5f08-928e-38af5abbd951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346345} {:answer/id #uuid "e58a7f26-eb29-5b9c-af1f-17e0f7406a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58a7f26-eb29-5b9c-af1f-17e0f7406a7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347373} :answer-field/id #uuid "34381abf-8ba3-5bc9-9655-c2fea1d1a76d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bfac1d8-b297-5aeb-80ab-63193055e75b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79672" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ respectively. What is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413699} {:answer/id #uuid "5176a3ef-a7e8-56ee-88b3-c87edbfb88f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413703} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5af234-fa4e-5f1f-bbf6-41796e869fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413706} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe1f55a-db1f-5973-81e4-e355d50f42c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413708} {:answer/id #uuid "c17a2582-0132-567b-8489-06c62880c92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413699} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7ef556-cad9-5361-bc68-0597551b8615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "450dd490-a7ea-5123-bd6e-a0e46d7aa855" :question/math-academy-id "q-79673" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. What is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}]}
